<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pompey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">106–<a href="/years/48-bce">48 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pompey was a Roman general and statesman of the first century BCE. During his eastern campaigns he annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 63 BCE. This ended the remnant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and made Syria a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first king known by name is Oroeses, who in 66–65 BCE attacked the Roman general <b>Pompey</b> during his Caucasian campaign and was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The final phase came in the Sertorian War of 80–72 BCE, when the Roman rebel Quintus Sertorius won the support of Lusitanians and Celtiberians and resisted the armies of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius and <b>Pompey</b> for several years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome conquered the empire in 69 BCE, and the defeat of Armenia's ally Mithridates VI of Pontus by <b>Pompey</b> at the Battle of the Lycus made the kingdom a Roman client in 66 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mithridates VI of Pontus quelled an uprising in the region in 83 BCE, and <b>Pompey</b> occupied Colchis after Mithridates' defeat in 65 BCE, capturing the local chief Olthaces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pompey the Great</b> fought his way into Iberia in 65 BCE while chasing Mithridates VI of Pontus, defeating King Artoces in battles on the Mtkvari and Aragvi rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Third Mithridatic War Lucullus broke his siege of Cyzicus in 73–72 BCE and defeated him at Cabira, and <b>Pompey</b> crushed his army on the Lycus river in 66 BCE, near the site where he then founded the city of Nicopolis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Regio V Picenum</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.05,32.6&amp;year=-69" aria-label="Pelusium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5393 1032 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Pompey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5843 1302h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5843 1302h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.05,32.6&amp;year=-69">What was here in 69 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What! will you never cease prating of laws to us that have swords by our sides?&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to the Mamertines of Messana, who pleaded their ancient privileges &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…whenever I stamp with my foot in any part of Italy, there will rise up forces enough in an instant, both horse and foot.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to those who asked what forces could stop Caesar, 50 BCE &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…more worshiped the rising than the setting sun…&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; Plutarch's account of his words to Sulla, who had refused him a triumph &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It behoves us, who are mortals born, to endure these events, and to try fortune yet again; neither is it any less possible to recover our former state, than it was to fall from that into this.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to his wife Cornelia after the defeat at Pharsalus; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
